**Read:** the practice tests in all three Math for Trades volumes and the relevant Prealgebra chapter reviews. Revisit the algebra and trigonometry selections where a wrong answer reveals an unfamiliar operation. Checking an answer is useful only after attempting and explaining it.

Use an unfamiliar small calculation sheet rather than reproducing memorized examples. Describe quantities and denominators, carry units through a compound conversion, solve an equation and a two-equation mixture, interpret a graph, estimate a volume, and resolve a sloping length into components. Explain why an apparently reasonable alternative answer fails.

**Work:** a hypothetical dry material arrives in 24 containers, each holding 18.5 kg net. A process retains 92% of that mass. Calculate receipt and retained mass, the loss, and the number of full 7 kg packages obtainable before allocating the remainder. Change the units to grams and verify the result. Receipt is 444 kg and retained mass is 408.48 kg; 58 full packages leave 2.48 kg. These supplied figures do not include packaging or cutting losses.

Draw a hypothetical rectangular tank, specify internal dimensions and a maximum filling fraction, and calculate operating volume in m³ and litres. Give a second estimate from a rough bounding shape. Write the same calculation as a table, an equation, and a short explanation a colleague can check.

**Ready to deepen:** demonstrate the reasoning without relying on an answer key, repair the cases that fail, and retain the corrected work. A person with existing fluency can use this review to enter the later material directly. The next readings use this foundation to interpret technical documents, quantities passing between organizations, and limits on what a calculation can establish.

**Continue:** [Technical drawings — turn views into a spatial description](../practice/Technical-drawings.lingua#r_3PEBG59JH06QGKQCR5YAKKF3R2).

